items.brief:
Symptom: the `frink_flows_define_stages` tool promises "multi-call DAG assembly (up to 50 stages per call)" and tells callers to "re-call with the full run list for failed stage numbers ... Existing runs for those stages will be replaced". The local implementation, `defineFlowBatchStages(flowId, batchId, stages)`, does none of that:
- a `dependsOn` entry naming a stage defined in an earlier call for the same batch is silently dropped, so that stage becomes an unintended root;
- a `dependsOn` entry naming a stage that exists nowhere is silently dropped too, and self-references, cycles and over-large fan-in are never rejected, so a cyclic batch simply never runs;
- calling again with a stage number that already exists fails on the unique (batch, stage number) constraint part-way through, after earlier stages of the same call were already written;
- `maxDepth` is just the number of stages in the call, and `rootStageCount` only looks at the current call.

Required behaviour of `defineFlowBatchStages(flowId, batchId, stages)` (exported from `src/main/lib/flows/mcp-cloud-shim.ts`; input items `{ stageNumber, name?, failureThreshold?, dependsOn?, runs: [{ triggerContext? }] }`):
1. The effective stage set is the batch's already-defined stages with the incoming stages merged in (an incoming stage replaces an existing stage with the same `stageNumber`). `dependsOn` numbers refer to stage numbers anywhere in that effective set. A missing or empty `dependsOn` means the stage has no dependencies (it is a root).
2. Before writing anything, the effective set is validated with these rules, checked in this order; the first rule the set breaks gives the error message:
   a. no two stages share a stage number: `Duplicate stageNumbers are not allowed` (this includes two incoming stages with the same `stageNumber` in one call);
   b. no stage depends on itself: `Stage <n> cannot depend on itself`;
   c. no stage lists the same stage number twice in its `dependsOn`: `Stage <n> has duplicate stageNumbers in dependsOn`;
   d. every `dependsOn` number is a stage in the effective set: `Stage <n> references non-existent stage <m>`;
   e. no stage has more than 10 dependencies: `Stage <n> exceeds maximum fan-in of 10 dependencies`;
   f. the dependencies form no cycle: `Cyclic dependency detected in stage graph`.
   Here `<n>` is the offending stage's number and `<m>` the missing stage number. If the set is invalid the call throws a `TRPCError` with code `BAD_REQUEST` whose message is exactly the message of the rule it breaks, and the database is left unchanged (no new stages, no new runs, no changed deps).
3. An incoming stage whose number already exists in the batch may only replace it while that existing stage's status is `pending`. Otherwise the whole call throws a `TRPCError` with code `CONFLICT` and message `Stage <n> has already started and cannot be redefined`, and nothing is written.
4. Replacing a pending stage keeps a single stage row for that number (same id), updates its name, failureThreshold and dependencies to the new values, and replaces its runs: afterwards the stage has exactly the runs given in this call, all `pending`.
5. New stages are created as today (status `pending`, their runs `pending`), with dependencies resolved to stage ids, including stages from earlier calls.
6. The return value keeps its shape: `stages` lists the incoming stages in input order with their stage row `id`, `stageNumber`, `name` (or null), `status: 'pending'`, `runCount` (number of runs given) and `dependsOnStageNumbers`; `rootStageCount` is the number of stages in the whole effective set with no dependencies; `maxDepth` is the length of the longest dependency chain in the whole effective set, counted in stages (a root has depth 1, and a stage's depth is one more than the largest depth among the stages it depends on).

Interface: `defineFlowBatchStages` from `src/main/lib/flows/mcp-cloud-shim.ts`, `listStagesForBatch` and `setStageStatusIf` (to mark a stage `running`) from `src/main/lib/db/repos/batch-stages.ts`, `listRunsForStage` from `src/main/lib/db/repos/batch-stage-runs.ts`, the stage row fields `id`, `stageNumber`, `name`, `status`, `failureThreshold`, `dependsOnStageIds`, and `TRPCError` from `@trpc/server`.

Symptom: the approval block is described everywhere as "Pause until approved or rejected" (block registry) and "pauses the run until the user approves/rejects in Frink" (Flow guidelines), but the local engine only supports approving, retrying or skipping a paused step (`resumeFlowRun` actions `'approve' | 'retry' | 'skip'`, mirrored by the `flows.resumeRun` tRPC procedure). There is no way to reject: a person who does not want the work to continue can only skip the approval (which lets the flow carry on) or cancel the run (which does not record that the approval was refused).

Required behaviour: a new export `rejectFlowRunApproval(flowRunId: string, nodeRunId: string, reason?: string): Promise<void>` from `src/main/lib/flows/resume.ts`, and a `flows.rejectRun` tRPC mutation (input `{ runId: string, nodeRunId: string, reason?: string }`, returning the same run-with-node-runs shape `flows.resumeRun` returns) that calls it.
1. Preconditions, each a `TRPCError`, checked before anything is written:
   - unknown run: code `NOT_FOUND`, message `Flow run not found`;
   - run not `paused`: code `PRECONDITION_FAILED`, message `Flow run is <status>; rejecting requires paused state.`;
   - node run missing or belonging to another run: code `NOT_FOUND`, message `Node run not found for this flow run`;
   - node run whose `blockType` is not `approval`, or whose status is not `awaiting_input`: code `PRECONDITION_FAILED`, message `Only an approval step waiting for a decision can be rejected`.
2. On success the rejection message is `Rejected` when `reason` is missing or blank, otherwise `Rejected: <reason trimmed>`, and:
   - the approval node run's status becomes `failed`, with its stored output's `error.message` equal to the rejection message (`retryable: false`);
   - the flow run's status becomes `failed` and `completedAt` is set;
   - every other node run of the run that is still active is cancelled, and the run's flow-linked tasks (`tasks.flowRunId` = the run) whose status is `pending` or `running` become `cancelled`;
   - exactly one `run_failed` flow event is emitted for the run, with `summary` equal to the rejection message;
   - no node after the approval step is dispatched.
3. Approve, retry and skip keep working exactly as today.

Interface: `rejectFlowRunApproval` and `resumeFlowRun` from `src/main/lib/flows/resume.ts`; `flowsRouter` from `src/main/lib/trpc/routers/flows` called through `flowsRouter.createCaller({ getWindow: () => null }).rejectRun(...)` (the result is read as `id`, `status`, `completed_at` and `nodeRuns[]` with `id`, `status` and `node_output.error.message`; an engine `TRPCError` reaches the caller with its code and message unchanged); `subscribeFlowEvents` from `src/main/lib/flows/events.ts` (events read as `eventType`, `flowRunId`, `summary`); `setFlowAdmissionLifecycleHooks` from `src/main/lib/flows/admission/activity.ts`; `TRPCError` from `@trpc/server`; the fixtures `seedFlowRun` / `seedCompletedNodeRun` from `src/main/lib/db/test-utils/flow-fixtures.ts` and `freshDb` from `src/main/lib/db/test-utils/fresh-db.ts`; the repos `getFlowRun`, `getOrCreateFlowRunByIdempotencyKey`, `setFlowRunStatus` (flow-runs), `createNodeRun`, `getNodeRun`, `listNodeRunsForFlowRun` (node-runs), `createTask`, `getTaskById` (tasks) under `src/main/lib/db/repos/`; and the `flow_runs` (`status`, `completedAt`) / `node_runs` (`nodeId`, `blockType`, `status`, `nodeOutput`) / `tasks` (`status`, `flowRunId`) rows. Limits on the new code: it reaches the database only through `getDatabase()` from `src/main/lib/db` (an in-memory `freshDb` database behaves the same); it uses nothing from `src/main/lib/flows/dispatch` other than `dispatchNode`, nothing from `src/main/lib/socket/executor` other than `pauseActiveExecutionForSubChat`, and nothing from `electron` beyond `app.getPath`; and it must work when, in `src/main/lib/flows/admission/runtime.ts`, `hasActiveFlowAdmission` and `hasPromotedFlowAdmission` report true and `cancelUndispatchedFlowAdmission` and `requestFlowAdmissionRelease` do nothing. A node run id that does not exist gets the same `NOT_FOUND` error as one from another run.
